OSKit INET — Inbound UDP + ICMP-error parsing + diag tests
==========================================================

Same-module sibling of `inet.lsysl`. Owns the inbound side of
UDP datagrams and the ICMP Destination Unreachable / Frag-Needed
parsers that surface peer-reported send errors back to userspace
sockets and update the PMTU cache. Test-only handlers that drive
those parsers (synthetic ICMP injection, PMTU seeding, frag-egress
counter, TCP data-segment emit counter) live alongside the code
under test for review locality.

    module oskit.servers

    import oskit.ipc.{ipc_reply}
    import std.net.{ipv4, ipv4_octet, IP_PROTO_UDP, IP_PROTO_TCP,
        ICMP_DEST_UNREACH, ICMP_CODE_FRAG_NEEDED,
        pkt_read_u16, pkt_match_ipv4}


### UDP — inbound

Dispatch an IPv4/UDP frame that already passed the destination-IP
check. Pulls header fields, looks up a socket bound to `dst_port`,
and either hands the datagram to a waiting recvfrom or enqueues it.
Dropping on no-match matches standard UDP semantics.

    inet_handle_udp(frame: *byte, total_len: int, ihl_bytes: int)
        val udp_off = 14 + ihl_bytes
        if total_len < udp_off + 8
            return
        val src_port = pkt_read_u16(frame, udp_off)
        val dst_port = pkt_read_u16(frame, udp_off + 2)
        val udp_len = pkt_read_u16(frame, udp_off + 4)
        if udp_len < 8
            return
        if total_len < udp_off + udp_len
            return
        val payload_len = udp_len - 8
        val data: *byte = frame + udp_off + 8
        val src_ip = ipv4(int(frame[14 + 12]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 13]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 14]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 15]) & 0xff)
        // Read dst from the wire rather than hardcoding our own IP:
        // broadcast UDP (DHCP OFFER/ACK) arrives with dst=255.255.255.255
        // and the checksum must pseudo-header it accordingly.
        val dst_ip = ipv4(int(frame[14 + 16]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 17]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 18]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 19]) & 0xff)
        if inet_udp_verify(src_ip, dst_ip, frame, udp_off, udp_len) == 0
            return
        val target = inet_find_bound_port(dst_port)
        if target < 0
            // No socket bound — emit ICMP Port Unreachable so the
            // peer's connect()ed UDP fd can return -ECONNREFUSED
            // immediately instead of timing out (Linux semantics).
            // Skip when the destination wasn't unicast to us — RFC
            // 1812 §4.3.2.7 forbids ICMP errors for broadcast /
            // multicast / 0.0.0.0 destinations to prevent
            // amplification.  Slix only owns one IP, so we check
            // dst_ip against ours and bail otherwise.
            if pkt_match_ipv4(frame, 14 + 16, INET_OUR_IP_A,
                              INET_OUR_IP_B, INET_OUR_IP_C,
                              INET_OUR_IP_D) != 0
                inet_send_icmp_port_unreach(frame, total_len)
            return
        if inet_sockets[target].pending_tid >= 0
            inet_deliver_to_waiter(target, src_ip, src_port, data, payload_len)
        else
            inet_enqueue(target, src_ip, src_port, data, payload_len)


### Inbound ICMP Destination Unreachable → connected UDP socket / PMTU update

Pairs with `inet_send_icmp_port_unreach`: when a peer reports our
UDP datagram couldn't be delivered, surface the error on the
socket that originated the send. RFC 792 §3.4 puts the offending
frame's IP header + first 8 bytes of L4 in the ICMP body, so we
parse the inner UDP header to find our local source port and look
the socket up by it.

Code 4 (RFC 1191 "Fragmentation Needed and DF Set") is handled
separately ahead of the proto-specific path: the ICMP body's
Next-Hop MTU goes into the per-destination PMTU cache so the
next emit to the inner-IP destination shrinks accordingly. This
applies whether the offending datagram was UDP, TCP, or ICMP —
PMTU is a routing fact, not a protocol fact.

We only handle UDP for the ECONNREFUSED path — the inner-protocol
check skips TCP and others. TCP error consumption (RST-on-ICMP)
is its own piece of work; the UDP path is what unblocks the typical
`connect()` / `sendto()` / `recvfrom()` loop returning
`-ECONNREFUSED` instead of timing out.

We don't validate the inner peer address against any
connect()ed peer — Linux does, but slix's UDP is permissive
enough that local-port match is good enough. A pathological
program with multiple sendto destinations from the same local
port may flag the wrong one, but that's bounded scope.

    val ICMP_ERRNO_ECONNREFUSED = 111

    inet_handle_icmp_dest_unreach(frame: *byte, len: int, icmp_off: int)
        val body_off = icmp_off + 8
        if len < body_off + 20
            return
        val inner_ihl = (int(frame[body_off]) & 0x0f) * 4
        if inner_ihl < 20
            return
        val icmp_code = int(frame[icmp_off + 1]) & 0xff
        if icmp_code == ICMP_CODE_FRAG_NEEDED
            // RFC 1191 §3 wire format: Next-Hop MTU lives in the
            // last 2 bytes of the ICMP header (icmp_off+6..7).
            // Older RFC 792 routers leave it 0; in that case we
            // skip the cache update — RFC 1191 §5 says the host
            // should fall back to a guessed plateau (576 etc.)
            // but slix doesn't, since the only entry point to
            // populate the cache today is this path.
            val mtu = pkt_read_u16(frame, icmp_off + 6)
            if mtu > 0
                val inner_dst = ipv4(
                    int(frame[body_off + 16]) & 0xff,
                    int(frame[body_off + 17]) & 0xff,
                    int(frame[body_off + 18]) & 0xff,
                    int(frame[body_off + 19]) & 0xff)
                inet_pmtu_set(inner_dst, mtu)
            return
        val inner_proto = int(frame[body_off + 9]) & 0xff
        if inner_proto != IP_PROTO_UDP
            return
        val inner_udp_off = body_off + inner_ihl
        if len < inner_udp_off + 8
            return
        val inner_src_port = pkt_read_u16(frame, inner_udp_off + 0)
        val target = inet_find_bound_port(inner_src_port)
        if target < 0
            return
        inet_sockets[target].pending_error = ICMP_ERRNO_ECONNREFUSED
        // If a recvfrom is parked on this socket, deliver the error
        // immediately so the caller wakes up; otherwise the next
        // recvfrom will pick the error up from pending_error.
        if inet_sockets[target].pending_tid >= 0
            val waiter = inet_sockets[target].pending_tid
            inet_sockets[target].pending_tid = -1
            inet_sockets[target].pending_peek = 0
            inet_sockets[target].recv_deadline = 0
            inet_sockets[target].pending_error = 0
            var er: [10]byte
            er[0] = byte(2)
            ipc_reply(waiter, er, 10)
        // Wake any epoll waiter so a poll loop notices via EPOLLERR
        // (the user will then call recvfrom and get -ECONNREFUSED).
        inet_epoll_fire(1, target)


### Synthetic ICMP-error injection (test-only)

Drives `inet_handle_icmp_dest_unreach` from a test program without
needing a real on-the-wire ICMP error. Caller passes the local
UDP source port that originally sent the (fictitious) datagram;
we synthesize the minimal RFC 792 "outer ICMP + inner IP+UDP"
frame with that port at the inner UDP src offset, and feed it
through the real parser.

Wire layout:
- request: cmd(1) + local_port(2)         = 3 bytes
- reply:   status(1)                       = 1 byte (0 always; the
  parser is fire-and-forget — failures show up as -ECONNREFUSED
  not arriving on recvfrom)

    inet_handle_icmp_unreach_inject(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val local_port = inet_read_u16(buf, 1)
        // Layout (offset / size):
        //   0  / 14  ETH placeholder
        //  14 / 20  IPv4 outer (only IHL nibble matters)
        //  34 / 8   ICMP outer (type+code rest unused)
        //  42 / 20  inner IPv4 (proto field at +9 must be UDP)
        //  62 / 8   inner UDP   (src_port at +0 = our local port)
        // = 70 bytes total.
        var frame: [70]byte
        var k = 0
        while k < 70
            frame[k] = byte(0)
            k += 1
        frame[14] = byte(0x45)               // ver=4, IHL=5
        frame[34] = byte(ICMP_DEST_UNREACH)
        frame[35] = byte(3)                  // PORT_UNREACH
        frame[42] = byte(0x45)               // inner ver=4, IHL=5
        frame[42 + 9] = byte(IP_PROTO_UDP)   // inner protocol
        frame[42 + 20 + 0] = byte((local_port >> 8) & 0xff)
        frame[42 + 20 + 1] = byte(local_port & 0xff)
        // icmp_off = 14 + 20 = 34
        inet_handle_icmp_dest_unreach(frame, 70, 34)
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### Test-only: seed PMTU cache + read fragment-egress counter

`INET_CMD_PMTU_INJECT_TEST` writes a per-destination MTU cap that
`inet_send_udp` will respect on the next send to that
destination. Until ICMP-driven discovery lands (Session 2 of
Option B), this is the only way to populate the cache from
userspace. `INET_CMD_FRAG_EGRESS_COUNT` returns the running count
of IP fragments that the egress path has emitted — the
single-frame fast path doesn't bump it, so a test can do
"counter_before + send + counter_after" and assert that
fragmentation actually kicked in.

Wire layouts:

- PMTU_INJECT_TEST request: cmd(1) + dst_a(1) + dst_b(1) +
  dst_c(1) + dst_d(1) + mtu_be(2) = 7 bytes.
  reply: status(1) = 1 byte (0 always; the inserter clamps a
  bad MTU rather than refusing).

- FRAG_EGRESS_COUNT request: cmd(1) = 1 byte.
  reply: status(1) + count_be32(4) = 5 bytes.

    inet_handle_pmtu_inject_test(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val a = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        val b = int(buf[2]) & 0xff
        val c = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        val d = int(buf[4]) & 0xff
        val mtu = pkt_read_u16(buf, 5)
        inet_pmtu_set(ipv4(a, b, c, d), mtu)
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

    inet_handle_frag_egress_count(sender: int, _buf: *byte)
        val v = inet_frag_egress_count
        var reply: [5]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte((v >> 24) & 0xff)
        reply[2] = byte((v >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[3] = byte((v >>  8) & 0xff)
        reply[4] = byte( v        & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 5)


### Test-only: drive ICMP-Frag-Needed through the real parser

`INET_CMD_PMTU_INJECT_TEST` writes the cache directly; this
companion command instead exercises the *real* RFC 1191 path.
It synthesizes an outer ICMP type-3 code-4 frame whose body
carries an inner IPv4 header with `dst = (a, b, c, d)`, then
hands the frame to `inet_handle_icmp_dest_unreach`. After the
parser runs the reply also reports the post-update cache value
so the test can assert the real path populated the cache.

The synthesized inner IP destination is what we want to match
against — that's the address WE sent to (so for our cache
that's the *destination* of our sends). The inner-IP source
field is left zero; the parser doesn't consult it for this
code path.

Wire layout:
- request: cmd(1) + dst_a(1) + dst_b(1) + dst_c(1) + dst_d(1)
           + mtu_be(2) = 7 bytes.
- reply:   status(1) + cached_mtu_be(2) = 3 bytes.
           status==0 always; cached_mtu reflects the cache
           after the synthesized parse runs.

    inet_handle_pmtu_discover_inject(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val a = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        val b = int(buf[2]) & 0xff
        val c = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        val d = int(buf[4]) & 0xff
        val mtu = pkt_read_u16(buf, 5)
        // Layout (offset / size):
        //   0  / 14  ETH placeholder
        //  14 / 20  IPv4 outer (only IHL nibble matters)
        //  34 / 8   ICMP outer (type/code/checksum/unused/MTU)
        //  42 / 20  inner IPv4 (proto + dst at +16..+19)
        //  62 / 8   inner UDP   (so the rfc-792 §3.4 length is met)
        // = 70 bytes total.
        var frame: [70]byte
        var k = 0
        while k < 70
            frame[k] = byte(0)
            k += 1
        frame[14] = byte(0x45)               // ver=4, IHL=5
        frame[34] = byte(ICMP_DEST_UNREACH)
        frame[35] = byte(ICMP_CODE_FRAG_NEEDED)
        // ICMP body: bytes 4-5 unused (0), 6-7 = Next-Hop MTU.
        frame[34 + 6] = byte((mtu >> 8) & 0xff)
        frame[34 + 7] = byte(mtu & 0xff)
        frame[42] = byte(0x45)               // inner ver=4, IHL=5
        frame[42 + 9] = byte(IP_PROTO_UDP)   // inner protocol
        // Inner IP destination = the address we want the cache
        // entry against (RFC 792 §3.4: the body holds the IP
        // header of the offending datagram, whose dst is what
        // we sent to).
        frame[42 + 16] = byte(a)
        frame[42 + 17] = byte(b)
        frame[42 + 18] = byte(c)
        frame[42 + 19] = byte(d)
        inet_handle_icmp_dest_unreach(frame, 70, 34)
        val cached = inet_pmtu_get(ipv4(a, b, c, d))
        var reply: [3]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte((cached >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[2] = byte(cached & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 3)


### Test-only: read TCP data-segment emit counter

Returns the running count of segments that `inet_tcp_emit`
sent with non-zero `payload_len`. A test takes the delta
across a `send()` and divides the byte count by the delta to
sanity-check the effective MSS, or asserts the count meets a
lower bound (`bytes / pmtu_cap`) to confirm the PMTU clamp
forced a finer-grained drain.

Wire layout:
- request: cmd(1) = 1 byte
- reply:   status(1) + count_be32(4) = 5 bytes

    inet_handle_tcp_data_seg_count(sender: int, _buf: *byte)
        val v = inet_tcp_data_seg_emit_count
        var reply: [5]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte((v >> 24) & 0xff)
        reply[2] = byte((v >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[3] = byte((v >>  8) & 0xff)
        reply[4] = byte( v        & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 5)
